resultText:
The bug is in `createTasks` in `src/domain/tasks.ts:294-337`, specifically the second pass at lines 326-331:

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

**Trigger shape:** a spec that lists **its own `temp_id`** inside its own `depends_on` array, e.g.:

```json
{ "temp_id": "A", "title": "...", "depends_on": ["A"] }
```

**Why it slips past the "never literally repeats its own real id" framing:** the client never wrote the task's real id anywhere — that id doesn't exist yet when the batch is authored, it's generated inside `createTask` in the first pass (lines 314-324). The client only wrote the string `"A"`, its own *temp_id*, into its own `depends_on`. Nothing in the input looks self-referential in terms of real ids.

The failure happens in the resolution step: `tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref` first checks whether `ref` matches a temp_id from *any* spec in the batch — including the spec's own. Since `tempIdToTaskId.set(temp_id, task.id)` was populated for every spec (including itself) in the first pass, looking up `"A"` for spec A resolves to spec A's own freshly-created `task.id`. That makes `dependsOnTaskId === results[i].task_id`, and the call `addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId)` passes the same real id for both arguments — tripping `addDependency`'s self-dependency check in `src/domain/dependencies.ts:27`, even though the raw client JSON never mentions any real id twice.

## Где происходит баг

Функция: **`createTasks`** в `src/domain/tasks.ts:294` (строки 326–331).

## Как это происходит

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

Разрешение `ref` идёт через `tempIdToTaskId` — map, куда на первом проходе кладётся `temp_id → task.id` **для каждой** спеки батча, включая саму себя.

Если спека `i` указывает **собственный `temp_id`** в своём же `depends_on`, то:

```json
{ "temp_id": "A", "title": "...", "depends_on": ["A"] }
```

на первом проходе создаётся задача с реальным `id` (например `uuid-A`) и `tempIdToTaskId.set("A", "uuid-A")`.

На втором проходе для этой же спеки `ref = "A"` → `tempIdToTaskId.get("A")` возвращает `"uuid-A"` — тот же `results[i].task_id`. В итоге вызывается `addDependency(db, "uuid-A", "uuid-A")` → `"A task cannot depend on itself"`.

## Почему это "незаметный" self-dependency

Сама спека нигде не пишет реальный `task_id` (его ещё не существует на момент составления батча) — она пишет свой `temp_id`. Строка `depends_on: ["A"]` выглядит как обычная ссылка на "какую-то другую задачу batch'а", а не как явное самоссылание. Самоссылание проявляется только после разрешения `temp_id → real id` во втором проходе — то есть баг триггерится не буквальным повтором id, а тем, что клиент указал **temp_id самой этой спеки** как элемент её собственного `depends_on`.

Found it. `createTasks` in `src/domain/tasks.ts:294-337` is the batch-creating caller.

**Where the bug triggers:** `src/domain/tasks.ts:326-331`, the dependency-wiring pass of `createTasks`:

```ts
specs.forEach((spec, i) => {
  for (const ref of spec.depends_on ?? []) {
    const dependsOnTaskId = tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref;
    addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId);
  }
});
```

**Exact input shape that triggers it:** a single spec in the batch that assigns itself a `temp_id` and then lists that *same* `temp_id` string inside its own `depends_on` array, e.g.:

```json
{ "temp_id": "T1", "title": "...", "depends_on": ["T1"] }
```

Tasks are created first (lines 314-324), so `T1` is already resolved into `tempIdToTaskId` (mapping to the newly-created real task id) by the time the dependency pass runs. When that same spec's `depends_on: ["T1"]` is processed, `tempIdToTaskId.get("T1")` returns the real id of the very task the spec itself just became — i.e. `results[i].task_id`. So `dependsOnTaskId` ends up equal to `results[i].task_id`, and `addDependency` throws `"A task cannot depend on itself"`.

The client never wrote its own *real* task id anywhere (it can't — the id doesn't exist until `createTask` runs inside this call); it only wrote its own *temp_id* as a dependency reference. The bug is that `createTasks` doesn't distinguish "a spec referencing itself by temp_id" from "a spec referencing a sibling," so the self-reference only becomes visible — and only throws — after resolution, deep inside `addDependency`, rather than being caught (with a clearer error) at the point where the client input is actually shaped wrong.
